### High-energy searches

Single radio bursts have been observed from isolated highly magnetized neutron stars, with contemporaneous high-energy emission in some cases (CHIME/FRB Collaboration et al., 2020; Israel et al., 2021). Hence, we carried out a search for any associated X-ray and soft \\gamma-ray emission consistent with the location of 2XMM J104608.7-594306. We find no publicly-reported flare-like emission at the time of the 2XMM J104608.7-594306 radio burst from any of the all-sky high-energy monitors such as _Swift_-BAT (Krimm et al., 2013), _Fermi_-GBM (Meegan et al., 2009) and INTEGRAL (Jensen et al., 2003). 2XMM J104608.7-594306 was visible to _Fermi_-GBM at the time of the burst, and we derive a 3\\sigma upper-limit of F_{\\rm X}\\leq 5.0\\times 10^{-8} erg s{}^{-1} cm{}^{-2} for a 0.2 s burst-like emission in the 8-100 keV energy range, and a thermal spectrum with a temperature of 10 keV, properties similar to magnetar-like emission. We also searched for sub-threshold magnetar-like bursts in _Fermi_-GBM in a 2-month interval around the time of the radio burst and we find no credible bursts consistent with the location of 2XMM J104608.7-594306. Additionally, no magnetar

